# Guarded-patcher rollback backups (2026-07-11): the existing *.bak rule matches only
# files ending exactly in '.bak'; it misses suffixed variants like .w1bak / .w2bak /
# .w2b1bak / .w2b2bak (guarded-edit backups) and .bak2 .. .bak9 (numbered backups).
# These patterns catch both so rollback backups are never accidentally committed.
*.w[0-9]*bak
*.bak[0-9]

# Rollback backups written by guarded patch scripts (*.py.bak_YYYY-MM-DD)
*.bak_*

# Coverage run artifacts (2026-07-22). `pytest --cov` writes .coverage into the
# repository root, and the Continuous Integration command additionally writes
# coverage.xml. Neither was ignored, so the first time the exact CI command was run
# locally -- during the 2026-07-22 teardown-abort investigation -- `git status` showed
# an untracked `.coverage`, which the standard `git add -A` staging step would have
# swept into the next commit. These are regenerated by every run and are never
# meaningful to track.
#
# Deliberately NOT broad: these name the coverage artifacts exactly rather than
# ignoring a wildcard that could also hide a real file. tests/conftest.py records why
# that restraint matters -- data/raw/ being gitignored is precisely what blinded
# `git status` to test-induced cache pollution (see the guard added 2026-07-11).
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Teardown-abort diagnostic output (2026-07-22). The dispatch-only diagnostic
# workflow and scripts/diagnostics/probe_teardown_abort.py write every artifact --
# per-arm JSON results, per-arm logs, the resolved environment, GNU Debugger
# backtraces and any core dumps -- into this single directory, so one precise rule
# covers them all and no generic name such as result_*.json or env.txt is shadowed
# at the repository root.
diagnostics_out/
